1
00:00:00,020 --> 00:00:02,479
For the first time ever,

2
00:00:02,519 --> 00:00:06,977
the world court has found Myanmar guilty
of genocide.

3
00:00:07,017 --> 00:00:12,474
And this week, the leaders of the United
States of America,

4
00:00:12,514 --> 00:00:18,971
Russia and China sat at a single table, a
cyclone crossed our coast,

5
00:00:19,011 --> 00:00:22,969
and an Indian company won the Earthshot
Prize.

6
00:00:23,009 --> 00:00:27,967
These are the strongest current affairs of
November's third week.

7
00:00:28,007 --> 00:00:34,464
This is Mounika. I'll help you with these
current affairs with data points,

8
00:00:34,504 --> 00:00:38,962
diagrams and a practice question for every
current affair.

9
00:00:39,002 --> 00:00:43,100
So, let's begin our journey of current
affairs.

10
00:00:43,290 --> 00:00:48,813
And here is the map: five from the world
stage, four on Indian law,

11
00:00:48,853 --> 00:00:51,197
economy and security, three from
environment,

12
00:00:51,237 --> 00:00:57,003
our states and the important days, and
then important persons and places in news.

13
00:00:57,090 --> 00:00:59,443
The world court finds genocide.

14
00:00:59,483 --> 00:01:06,621
Story one: why is the world court's
finding of genocide by Myanmar a first in

15
00:01:06,661 --> 00:01:07,390
history?

16
00:01:07,430 --> 00:01:11,781
On November 19th, the International Court
of Justice ruled,

17
00:01:11,821 --> 00:01:14,221
by nine votes to six,

18
00:01:14,261 --> 00:01:20,076
that Myanmar committed genocide against
the Rohingya during its 2017 military

19
00:01:20,116 --> 00:01:22,028
campaign in Rakhine State,

20
00:01:22,068 --> 00:01:25,931
in which about 10,000 Rohingya were
killed.

21
00:01:25,971 --> 00:01:33,250
It is the first time the court has found a
state itself responsible for genocide,

22
00:01:33,290 --> 00:01:39,105
and it ordered Myanmar to begin a
programme of restitution and compensation

23
00:01:39,145 --> 00:01:42,560
and to report back within two years.

24
00:01:42,600 --> 00:01:46,461
How did a small African country bring this
case?

25
00:01:46,501 --> 00:01:49,495
The Gambia filed it in 2019,

26
00:01:49,535 --> 00:01:55,130
on behalf of the 57 members of the
Organisation of Islamic Cooperation.

27
00:01:55,170 --> 00:02:02,065
It could do so because the Genocide
Convention of 1948 lets any party take

28
00:02:02,105 --> 00:02:07,740
another party to the world court, even if
it was not harmed itself.

29
00:02:07,780 --> 00:02:10,474
Now the exam angle for India.

30
00:02:10,514 --> 00:02:14,304
India ratified the Genocide Convention in
1959,

31
00:02:14,615 --> 00:02:16,854
and it hosts Rohingya refugees,

32
00:02:16,894 --> 00:02:22,778
though it is not a party to the 1951
Refugee Convention.

33
00:02:22,818 --> 00:02:25,512
So this judgment touches our
neighbourhood,

34
00:02:25,552 --> 00:02:29,157
our refugee policy and our relations with
Myanmar,

35
00:02:29,197 --> 00:02:33,899
which shares a sixteen-hundred-kilometre
border with our north-east.

36
00:02:34,250 --> 00:02:39,238
Prelims trap: the International Court of
Justice judges states,

37
00:02:39,278 --> 00:02:44,825
not individuals; individuals are tried by
the International Criminal Court,

38
00:02:44,865 --> 00:02:49,853
and India is not a member of that court.

39
00:02:49,893 --> 00:02:51,010
Remember this.

40
00:02:51,050 --> 00:02:51,350
Your first question: how many of these
three statements are correct?

41
00:02:57,650 --> 00:03:00,819
The answer is B, only two.

42
00:03:00,859 --> 00:03:06,167
The world court has 15 judges with
nine-year terms,

43
00:03:06,207 --> 00:03:10,981
and India is a party to the Genocide
Convention;

44
00:03:11,021 --> 00:03:14,295
but the court cannot try individuals.

45
00:03:14,520 --> 00:03:17,979
America, China and Russia at a single
table.

46
00:03:18,019 --> 00:03:23,229
Story two: the leaders of the nuclear
powers, Russia, America and China,

47
00:03:23,269 --> 00:03:25,416
sat at a single table.

48
00:03:25,456 --> 00:03:27,603
What did they talk about?

49
00:03:27,643 --> 00:03:29,993
What did they agree on?

50
00:03:30,120 --> 00:03:32,659
On November 19th, Donald Trump,

51
00:03:32,699 --> 00:03:39,879
Xi Jinping and Vladimir Putin held their
first-ever three-way summit in Shenzhen,

52
00:03:39,919 --> 00:03:45,551
on the sidelines of the Asia-Pacific
Economic Cooperation leaders' meeting.

53
00:03:45,591 --> 00:03:51,224
Their joint declaration promised a
dialogue on reducing nuclear risks and

54
00:03:51,264 --> 00:03:54,319
stopping the spread of nuclear weapons,

55
00:03:54,359 --> 00:03:59,000
but it set no deadline and no disarmament
target.

56
00:03:59,040 --> 00:04:00,657
Why does this matter?

57
00:04:00,697 --> 00:04:05,216
The last treaty limiting American and
Russian nuclear weapons, New START,

58
00:04:05,256 --> 00:04:07,287
lapsed in February this year,

59
00:04:07,327 --> 00:04:11,846
so for now no treaty caps the world's two
largest arsenals.

60
00:04:11,886 --> 00:04:18,930
A three-way dialogue that includes China
is new, but it is only a promise to talk.

61
00:04:18,970 --> 00:04:23,733
For India, all three are vital: America
for technology and the Quad,

62
00:04:23,773 --> 00:04:25,734
Russia for defence and oil,

63
00:04:25,774 --> 00:04:29,736
and China as our biggest neighbour and a
trading partner.

64
00:04:29,776 --> 00:04:31,737
When the three talk directly,

65
00:04:31,777 --> 00:04:36,980
India must keep its strategic autonomy and
its own seat at the table.

66
00:04:37,020 --> 00:04:41,571
Prelims trap: the Non-Proliferation Treaty
recognises only five nuclear-weapon

67
00:04:41,849 --> 00:04:47,954
states, America, Russia, Britain, France
and China; India is not a party to it,

68
00:04:47,994 --> 00:04:54,140
nor to the test-ban treaty, and follows a
policy of no first use.

69
00:04:54,180 --> 00:05:00,929
Question two: how many of these countries
are recognised as nuclear-weapon states

70
00:05:00,969 --> 00:05:03,570
under the Non-Proliferation Treaty?

71
00:05:09,870 --> 00:05:12,870
The answer is B, only two:

72
00:05:12,910 --> 00:05:19,963
China and France. India and Israel are not
recognised nuclear-weapon states under the

73
00:05:20,003 --> 00:05:20,760
treaty.

74
00:05:20,800 --> 00:05:23,192
The Quad meets in Manila.

75
00:05:23,232 --> 00:05:28,542
Story three: the Quad leaders have met
after two long years.

76
00:05:28,582 --> 00:05:31,614
What did India gain from that?

77
00:05:31,790 --> 00:05:34,187
On November 17th in Manila,

78
00:05:34,227 --> 00:05:40,189
the Quad leaders backed an artificial
intelligence accelerator with more than 85

79
00:05:40,564 --> 00:05:47,348
million dollars, agreed to hold a Quad
Business Leaders' Forum in Mumbai in early

80
00:05:47,388 --> 00:05:52,223
2027, and stepped up cooperation on cyber
security,

81
00:05:52,263 --> 00:05:54,700
maritime surveillance and critical
minerals.

82
00:05:54,740 --> 00:05:57,189
In the same visit,

83
00:05:57,229 --> 00:06:07,147
the Prime Minister attended the 23rd
ASEAN–India Summit and the 21st East Asia

84
00:06:07,187 --> 00:06:15,255
Summit, which keeps our Act East Policy at
the centre of the Indo-Pacific.

85
00:06:15,940 --> 00:06:19,103
Prelims trap: India is a member of the
Quad and of the East Asia Summit,

86
00:06:19,143 --> 00:06:21,452
but only a partner of ASEAN, which now has
11 members,

87
00:06:21,492 --> 00:06:23,200
and not a member of APEC at all.

88
00:06:23,240 --> 00:06:27,290
Question three: how many of these
groupings is India a member of?

89
00:06:33,590 --> 00:06:41,168
The answer is B, only two: the Quad and
the East Asia Summit.

90
00:06:41,440 --> 00:06:45,497
India says no to a death-penalty pause.

91
00:06:45,537 --> 00:06:52,667
Story four: why did India vote against the
United Nations' pause on the death

92
00:06:52,707 --> 00:06:55,868
penalty? Why did it do so?

93
00:06:56,070 --> 00:06:57,505
On November 13th,

94
00:06:57,545 --> 00:07:03,404
the United Nations General Assembly
adopted a resolution calling for a global

95
00:07:03,444 --> 00:07:10,286
moratorium on the death penalty, with a
133 votes in favour,

96
00:07:10,326 --> 00:07:13,236
33 against and 16 abstentions.

97
00:07:13,276 --> 00:07:17,660
India voted against it, as it has every
time,

98
00:07:17,700 --> 00:07:24,050
saying there is no international consensus
and that each country has the sovereign

99
00:07:24,090 --> 00:07:27,040
right to decide its own laws.

100
00:07:27,080 --> 00:07:30,854
At home, the death penalty survives within
strict limits.

101
00:07:30,894 --> 00:07:37,636
Article 21 allows the state to take life
only by a procedure established by law,

102
00:07:37,676 --> 00:07:40,602
and in Bachan Singh in 1980,

103
00:07:40,642 --> 00:07:46,960
the Supreme Court said it should be used
only in the rarest of rare cases.

104
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:54,589
After that, the President under Article 72
and the Governor under Article 161 can

105
00:07:54,629 --> 00:07:55,900
still grant pardon.

106
00:07:55,940 --> 00:08:01,011
Prelims trap: only the President can
pardon a sentence passed by a

107
00:08:01,051 --> 00:08:07,400
court-martial; the Governor's power under
Article 161 does not extend to

108
00:08:07,440 --> 00:08:09,570
court-martial sentences. Remember this.

109
00:08:09,610 --> 00:08:16,565
Question four: consider these two
statements and choose the right option.

110
00:08:22,890 --> 00:08:26,207
The answer is A: both are correct,

111
00:08:26,247 --> 00:08:32,601
and Article 72 is exactly why the
President can pardon a court-martial

112
00:08:32,960 --> 00:08:33,690
sentence.

113
00:08:33,730 --> 00:08:36,392
One year of the Labour Codes.

114
00:08:36,432 --> 00:08:42,698
Story five: it has been one year since
India brought in the Labour Codes.

115
00:08:42,738 --> 00:08:44,690
What changed with them?

116
00:08:44,830 --> 00:08:47,763
On November 21st, 2025,

117
00:08:48,097 --> 00:08:54,123
four labour codes came into force and
replaced 29 central labour laws:

118
00:08:54,163 --> 00:08:57,857
the Code on Wages, the Industrial
Relations Code,

119
00:08:57,897 --> 00:09:02,057
the Code on Social Security, and the
Occupational Safety,

120
00:09:02,097 --> 00:09:04,430
Health and Working Conditions Code.

121
00:09:04,470 --> 00:09:09,432
Workers gain a national floor wage, and,
for the first time,

122
00:09:09,472 --> 00:09:15,343
gig and platform workers are defined in
law and brought under social security.

123
00:09:15,383 --> 00:09:18,981
Employers gain simpler compliance and
fixed-term hiring.

124
00:09:19,021 --> 00:09:22,164
Some provisions are still being phased in,

125
00:09:22,204 --> 00:09:27,660
so the real test is how states write and
enforce their rules.

126
00:09:27,700 --> 00:09:30,683
Prelims trap: labour is in the Concurrent
List,

127
00:09:30,723 --> 00:09:34,841
so both Parliament and the states can make
laws on it,

128
00:09:34,881 --> 00:09:38,660
and states must frame their own rules
under these codes.

129
00:09:38,700 --> 00:09:43,600
Question five: how many of these
statements are correct?

130
00:09:49,900 --> 00:09:52,541
The answer is B, only two.

131
00:09:52,581 --> 00:09:57,203
Labour is in the Concurrent List, not the
Union List.

132
00:09:57,340 --> 00:10:04,071
So you are still listening, which means
you are really a serious aspirant,

133
00:10:04,111 --> 00:10:10,322
eager to learn. Okay, now let's come back
to India: our coast,

134
00:10:10,362 --> 00:10:15,129
our problems and the Indian topics in this
session.

135
00:10:15,340 --> 00:10:19,471
India's defence week. Story six: this
week,

136
00:10:19,511 --> 00:10:26,026
India held defence talks and defence
exercises with four different countries.

137
00:10:26,066 --> 00:10:28,454
Let's line them up.

138
00:10:28,740 --> 00:10:33,571
With Japan, the defence and foreign
ministers held the fourth two-plus-two

139
00:10:34,240 --> 00:10:38,430
dialogue in Tokyo. With Malaysia, the
armies began Harimau Shakti,

140
00:10:38,470 --> 00:10:41,186
focused on jungle warfare and
counter-insurgency.

141
00:10:41,432 --> 00:10:46,045
With Sri Lanka, the 12th Mitra Shakti
exercise is under way,

142
00:10:46,085 --> 00:10:49,007
focused on counter-terrorism. And with
Israel,

143
00:10:49,047 --> 00:10:57,468
the Defence Ministry signed a contract of
about 1,976 crore rupees to upgrade the

144
00:10:57,508 --> 00:10:59,200
Navy's Barak missile defence.

145
00:10:59,240 --> 00:11:04,564
A two-plus-two means foreign and defence
ministers meeting together,

146
00:11:04,604 --> 00:11:08,465
a format India keeps for its closest
partners,

147
00:11:08,505 --> 00:11:15,445
and with Japan it links straight to our
Act East Policy and the Indo-Pacific.

148
00:11:15,860 --> 00:11:22,180
Question six: how many of these exercise
pairs are correctly matched?

149
00:11:28,480 --> 00:11:32,169
The answer is C, only three.

150
00:11:32,209 --> 00:11:36,538
Garuda Shakti is with Indonesia, not
Malaysia.

151
00:11:36,850 --> 00:11:39,595
Cyclone Akvan crosses our coast.

152
00:11:39,635 --> 00:11:46,836
Story seven: Cyclone Akvan crossed the
northern part of Andhra Pradesh and the

153
00:11:46,876 --> 00:11:50,178
southern part of Odisha on November 20.

154
00:11:50,218 --> 00:11:53,603
So everyone, please be very careful.

155
00:11:53,850 --> 00:11:58,095
It crossed close to Gopalpur in Odisha's
Ganjam district,

156
00:11:58,135 --> 00:12:04,762
with a red alert for Srikakulam and
Vizianagaram and more than 3 lakh people

157
00:12:04,802 --> 00:12:06,230
moved to safety.

158
00:12:06,270 --> 00:12:11,143
The name Akvan was given by Iran, from a
demon in the Persian epic,

159
00:12:11,183 --> 00:12:17,461
the Shahnameh. 13 countries gave a 169
names in 2020, used strictly in order,

160
00:12:17,501 --> 00:12:20,619
and India's weather office in New Delhi
assigns them.

161
00:12:20,659 --> 00:12:22,725
Storms are graded by wind speed:

162
00:12:22,765 --> 00:12:28,278
a cyclonic storm starts at 62 kilometres
an hour, a severe one at 89,

163
00:12:28,380 --> 00:12:31,890
and a very severe one at 118.

164
00:12:31,930 --> 00:12:37,561
Prelims trap: Yanam sits on the Andhra
coast, but it belongs to Puducherry,

165
00:12:37,601 --> 00:12:38,910
not Andhra Pradesh.

166
00:12:38,950 --> 00:12:43,500
Question seven: how many of these
statements are correct?

167
00:12:49,800 --> 00:12:51,674
The answer is B, only two.

168
00:12:51,714 --> 00:12:56,430
Names are used in a fixed order, not by
the country a storm hits.

169
00:12:56,470 --> 00:13:02,635
Telugu States: the Nagarjuna Sagar water
row.

170
00:13:02,962 --> 00:13:07,272
Now, our Telugu states story.

171
00:13:07,890 --> 00:13:10,470
Nagarjuna Sagar sits on the Krishna,

172
00:13:10,510 --> 00:13:15,274
on the border of Telangana's Nalgonda and
Andhra Pradesh's Palnadu district,

173
00:13:15,314 --> 00:13:17,895
and both states draw from it.

174
00:13:17,935 --> 00:13:22,699
This week, officials of the two states
clashed over water releases,

175
00:13:22,739 --> 00:13:29,290
and with a dry year ahead, every drop for
drinking water is being argued over.

176
00:13:29,330 --> 00:13:32,120
Who decides? The Krishna River Management
Board,

177
00:13:32,160 --> 00:13:36,567
set up under the Andhra Pradesh
Reorganisation Act of 2014,

178
00:13:36,607 --> 00:13:40,206
manages releases. The shares come from
tribunals: the first,

179
00:13:40,246 --> 00:13:44,080
under Justice Bachawat, gave its award in
1976,

180
00:13:44,289 --> 00:13:47,887
and the second is headed by Justice
Brijesh Kumar.

181
00:13:47,927 --> 00:13:54,800
And under Article 262, Parliament can even
keep the courts out of such disputes.

182
00:13:54,840 --> 00:14:00,390
Question eight, for Group-1 too: how many
of these statements are correct?

183
00:14:06,690 --> 00:14:12,172
The answer is C, all three are correct.

184
00:14:12,560 --> 00:14:15,961
An Indian company wins the Earthshot
Prize.

185
00:14:16,001 --> 00:14:19,401
Story nine: for the first time ever,

186
00:14:19,441 --> 00:14:25,299
the Earthshot Prize function was held in
India, in our own Mumbai,

187
00:14:25,339 --> 00:14:28,888
and an Indian company has won it.

188
00:14:29,070 --> 00:14:35,244
The prize was launched in 2020 by Prince
William and his Royal Foundation,

189
00:14:35,284 --> 00:14:40,571
and every year it gives 1 million pounds
each to five winners.

190
00:14:40,611 --> 00:14:45,050
Its sixth edition was held in Mumbai on
November 17th.

191
00:14:45,090 --> 00:14:51,410
Mahindra Last Mile Mobility won Clean Our
Air for affordable electric autos,

192
00:14:51,450 --> 00:14:55,650
with more than 4 lakh on Indian roads.

193
00:14:55,690 --> 00:14:57,770
A very proud moment!

194
00:14:57,810 --> 00:15:03,070
The others were Bhutan, the first country
ever to win,

195
00:15:03,110 --> 00:15:07,310
the Pacific island of Niue for ocean
protection,

196
00:15:07,350 --> 00:15:10,490
a French campaign against forever
chemicals,

197
00:15:10,530 --> 00:15:16,890
and Denmark's Ørsted for moving from oil
and gas to offshore wind.

198
00:15:16,930 --> 00:15:21,917
Prelims trap: the Earthshot Prize is not a
United Nations award;

199
00:15:21,957 --> 00:15:27,401
the UN's own top green honour is Champions
of the Earth,

200
00:15:27,441 --> 00:15:30,640
given by the UN Environment Programme.

201
00:15:30,680 --> 00:15:32,285
Question nine: match the winners.

202
00:15:32,325 --> 00:15:33,960
How many pairs are correct?

203
00:15:40,260 --> 00:15:43,569
The answer is B, only two.

204
00:15:43,609 --> 00:15:49,232
Niue won Revive Our Oceans; Ørsted won Fix
Our Climate.

205
00:15:49,480 --> 00:15:51,054
COP31 ends in Antalya.

206
00:15:51,094 --> 00:15:56,300
Story 10: the climate summit in Türkiye
has come to an end.

207
00:15:56,340 --> 00:16:02,186
Who was running it, what did they discuss,
and what did they agree on?

208
00:16:02,280 --> 00:16:04,228
"Australia · President of Negotiations
(Chris Bowen)";

209
00:16:04,268 --> 00:16:07,519
chip "Antalya · 9–20 Nov 2026 · decisions
need consensus".

210
00:16:21,540 --> 00:16:26,469
In the final hours, the latest draft,
called the Antalya Declaration,

211
00:16:26,509 --> 00:16:33,696
dropped every mention of moving away from
fossil fuels and a proposed target of 100

212
00:16:33,736 --> 00:16:36,406
and 20 billion dollars for adaptation,

213
00:16:36,446 --> 00:16:41,826
which disappointed more than 80 countries
that wanted a fossil-fuel roadmap.

214
00:16:41,866 --> 00:16:43,633
India's position stayed clear:

215
00:16:43,673 --> 00:16:48,150
developed countries must deliver the
finance they promised at Baku,

216
00:16:48,190 --> 00:16:53,307
at least 300 billion dollars a year by
2035.

217
00:16:53,650 --> 00:16:58,590
Question 10: how many pairs are correctly
matched?

218
00:17:04,900 --> 00:17:08,340
The answer is C, only three.

219
00:17:08,380 --> 00:17:16,520
COP 31 was held in Antalya, Türkiye;
Australia only led the negotiations.

220
00:17:16,790 --> 00:17:19,916
India hosts the SCO Civilisational
Dialogue.

221
00:17:19,956 --> 00:17:23,082
Story 11: for the first time,

222
00:17:23,122 --> 00:17:26,248
India is holding a civilisational
dialogue,

223
00:17:26,288 --> 00:17:30,582
something that has not been done till now.

224
00:17:30,800 --> 00:17:33,569
From November 20th to 22nd,

225
00:17:34,053 --> 00:17:38,892
New Delhi hosts the first SCO
Civilisational Dialogue Forum,

226
00:17:38,932 --> 00:17:45,496
which the Prime Minister proposed at the
Tianjin summit in 2025,

227
00:17:45,981 --> 00:17:50,860
with sessions on philosophy, art,
languages and living traditions.

228
00:17:50,900 --> 00:17:55,815
The organisation, founded in 2001, now has
10 members,

229
00:17:55,855 --> 00:17:59,158
with Belarus the newest in 2024.

230
00:17:59,459 --> 00:18:03,473
Prelims trap: Afghanistan, Mongolia and
Turkmenistan are not members,

231
00:18:03,513 --> 00:18:09,519
and since September, Pakistan chairs the
organisation for 2026 to 27.

232
00:18:09,860 --> 00:18:17,030
Question 11: how many of these are members
of the Shanghai Cooperation Organisation?

233
00:18:23,330 --> 00:18:27,814
The answer is B, only two: Belarus and
Iran.

234
00:18:27,990 --> 00:18:29,623
Important days of the week.

235
00:18:29,663 --> 00:18:32,969
Story 12: there are lots of important days
this week,

236
00:18:33,009 --> 00:18:36,270
and each one has a static part in it.

237
00:18:36,310 --> 00:18:38,828
November 16th was National Press Day,

238
00:18:38,868 --> 00:18:44,158
marking the start of the Press Council of
India in 1966,

239
00:18:44,410 --> 00:18:47,781
a statutory body that covers only print
media.

240
00:18:47,821 --> 00:18:53,324
November 19th was World Toilet Day, with
the theme safe toilets, equal futures,

241
00:18:53,364 --> 00:18:58,440
and it was also the birth anniversary of
Rani Lakshmibai of Jhansi,

242
00:18:58,480 --> 00:19:00,359
born in 46.

243
00:19:00,611 --> 00:19:03,556
And November 20th is World Children's Day,

244
00:19:03,596 --> 00:19:11,017
the day the UN Convention on the Rights of
the Child was adopted in 1989.

245
00:19:11,310 --> 00:19:15,940
Question 12: how many pairs are correctly
matched?

246
00:19:22,240 --> 00:19:25,964
The answer is C, only three.

247
00:19:26,004 --> 00:19:34,133
March 22nd is World Water Day; Global
Handwashing Day is October 15th.

248
00:19:34,450 --> 00:19:37,549
Persons, places and awards in news.

249
00:19:37,589 --> 00:19:43,828
Before we finish, let's discuss prominent
persons and places in the news;

250
00:19:43,868 --> 00:19:47,607
many questions are framed from this area.

251
00:19:47,820 --> 00:19:54,516
First, persons. Yuji Iwasawa of Japan is
the President of the International Court

252
00:19:54,556 --> 00:19:58,662
of Justice, who read out the Myanmar
judgment.

253
00:19:58,702 --> 00:20:04,362
Bhavika Mangalanandan explained India's
vote against the death-penalty pause at

254
00:20:04,402 --> 00:20:10,580
the United Nations. Chris Bowen of
Australia led the climate negotiations at

255
00:20:10,620 --> 00:20:16,320
Antalya. And Jack Sim of Singapore founded
the World Toilet Organization.

256
00:20:16,360 --> 00:20:21,763
Now places. Rakhine is the Myanmar state
the Rohingya come from.

257
00:20:21,803 --> 00:20:27,206
Shenzhen, next to Hong Kong, was China's
first special economic zone,

258
00:20:27,246 --> 00:20:32,154
in 1980. Gopalpur in Odisha is where Akvan
crossed.

259
00:20:32,194 --> 00:20:37,102
Antalya on Türkiye's Mediterranean coast
hosted the climate summit,

260
00:20:37,142 --> 00:20:41,100
and Delhi's Purana Qila hosts the SCO
forum.

261
00:20:41,140 --> 00:20:43,294
And awards and culture:

262
00:20:43,334 --> 00:20:50,972
the 57th International Film Festival of
India opened in Goa on November 20th,

263
00:20:51,012 --> 00:20:58,102
with Spain as the Country of Honour and
Russia as the Partner Country,

264
00:20:58,142 --> 00:21:02,530
and an Indian company won the Earthshot
Prize.

265
00:21:02,570 --> 00:21:07,380
Last question: how many pairs are
correctly matched?

266
00:21:13,680 --> 00:21:17,492
The answer is C, only three.

267
00:21:17,534 --> 00:21:21,988
Gopalpur is in Odisha, not Andhra Pradesh.

268
00:21:22,320 --> 00:21:24,723
For our Mains question this week:

269
00:21:24,763 --> 00:21:28,387
the world court has found Myanmar guilty
of genocide.

270
00:21:28,427 --> 00:21:30,830
We discussed it in class, right?

271
00:21:30,870 --> 00:21:35,716
So, what will this mean for international
law, and coming to India,

272
00:21:35,756 --> 00:21:38,566
what does it mean for our country?

273
00:21:38,606 --> 00:21:42,463
Let me know your answer in the comments
section.

274
00:21:42,560 --> 00:21:44,765
That's all for now.

275
00:21:44,805 --> 00:21:51,502
We discussed three big countries sitting
at a single table, the cyclone,

276
00:21:51,542 --> 00:21:56,554
and the Indian company that won the
Earthshot Prize.

277
00:21:56,594 --> 00:21:58,799
If this was helpful,

278
00:21:58,839 --> 00:22:04,974
share it with someone who is seriously
preparing for the exam,

279
00:22:05,014 --> 00:22:09,465
and we will meet you next Sunday at 6.

280
00:22:09,505 --> 00:22:11,789
Thank you. Keep watching.

